#!/usr/bin/python3
# jobstats_dcgm - per-job, time-averaged DCGM *profiling* metrics (SM-active,
# occupancy, tensor/fp pipe activity, DRAM-active, power, energy, ...) that
# jobstats does NOT store in its AdminComment blob. Companion to jobstats /
# jobstats_history / seff_history.
#
# jobstats only keeps ONE time-averaged GPU number -- nvidia_gpu_duty_cycle
# ("GPU%"), a coarse "a kernel was running, yes/no" duty cycle. The richer
# DCGM_FI_PROF_* fields (how hard the SMs / tensor cores actually worked) live
# in the same Prometheus but are never written to the blob. This pulls them
# straight from that endpoint for one or more JOBIDs.
#
# Endpoint + sampling period are taken from the jobstats `config` module
# (PROM_SERVER), so the credentials are never duplicated here.
#
# How a JOBID maps to GPUs: the jobstats exporter publishes nvidia_gpu_jobId per
# GPU; we read the set of GPU UUIDs that ran the job over its window, then join
# the DCGM_FI_* series ON UUID. (The DCGM `gpu` index and the slurm
# `minor_number` do NOT agree -- only the UUID is stable across the two
# exporters.) Each value is averaged over the job's [start,end] window via a
# PromQL subquery, exactly like jobstats does for the duty cycle.
#
# Author: Bala Desinghu, Senior AI/HPC Research Computing Engineer, Kempner Institute, Harvard

